(borge-bug (id "BORGE-1790987430")
  (title "prod DB has no idle_in_transaction_session_timeout / statement_timeout / lock_timeout — a leaked transaction blocks the whole tree indefinitely (2026-10-02 prod stall)")
  (status closed)
  (created 2026-10-02)
  (filed-by roerick)
  (doc "FOUND 2026-10-02, prod (pricklypear.rocks), investigating recurring
/api/eval timeouts. Same failure twice that day: ~20:46Z and ~23:38Z.

ROOT CAUSE: the production database has NO server-side safety net against a
transaction that is opened and then abandoned. PostgreSQL 18.4, all four
guards disabled (defaults, source=default):

    idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = 0   (disabled)
    statement_timeout                    = 0   (disabled)
    lock_timeout                         = 0   (disabled)
    idle_session_timeout                 = 0   (disabled)

At 23:38:41Z a backend (pid 572) ran BEGIN + one SELECT on tree_nodes and then
went `idle in transaction` for 30+ minutes holding transactionid 3255420. With
idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = 0, Postgres NEVER reclaims it. It blocked:

    pid 572  idle in transaction, xid 3255420   (the leaked reader)
      \\-> pid 93064  UPDATE tree_nodes  (active, 26 min, Lock/transactionid)
          \\-> pid 91066  UPDATE tree_nodes  (active, 22 min, Lock/tuple)

Confirmed with pg_blocking_pids(). Because the image serializes DB work through
one pgx connection per identity (image.per-identity-sessions), every subsequent
eval and page route queued behind the stuck writers -> 499s and 504s. /health
stayed green the whole time (see BORGE-1790987437).

The only recovery was `pg_terminate_backend(572)` / an image restart. A
10-minute idle_in_transaction_session_timeout would have self-cleared it; a
statement_timeout would have bounded the runaway writes; a lock_timeout would
have surfaced the wait as an error instead of an indefinite hang.

FIX: set role-level (ALTER ROLE pricklypear SET ...) or postgresql.conf:
  - idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = e.g. 60000-300000 ms
  - statement_timeout = a generous but finite bound (the image has
    long-running maintenance queries; measure first)
  - lock_timeout = finite, so a blocked writer errors instead of hanging
Record in borge/single-image.borg or borge/image.borg runtime section.

VERIFY: \d+ role shows the settings; a synthetic idle-in-tx session is
terminated by Postgres after the timeout; pg_stat_activity has no long
idle-in-transaction lenders under load.
")
  (resolution (when 1790988387) (how closed) (by roerick))
)
